  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Let $V=C[-1,0],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,0].$ Find $\u27e8t,\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\u27e9$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\u27e8x(t),y(t)\u27e9={\\int }_{-1}^{0}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n\u27e8t,\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\u27e9={\\int }_{-1}^{0}t\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=1-{t}^{2},$ $\\text{d}u=-2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}t\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3/2}]-[-\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
